{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-121517" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which integral gives the area of the finite region bounded between $y=8{x}^{2}-4$ and $y=2-2x?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8c84cfd58ece715554e171ab022d8fa0e5246d1cf79827113007fe1452df3bc"} {"question" "q-121517" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/assets/b2f34492ce184169d0e720d5a2214a356236b56d6e20bd8ece584b967f074e64.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-2x & =8{x}^{2}-4 \\\\ 8{x}^{2}+2x-6 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{{2}^{2}-4\\cdot 8\\cdot (-6)}}{2\\cdot 8} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\pm 14}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-1$ to $x=\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/assets/94243d27b465eca7957731385b2fe13fae36b811079d3077e2e093aa19d81ad3.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=8{x}^{2}-4,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=2-2x.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3/4}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3/4}[\\,(2-2x)-(8{x}^{2}-4)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3/4}(-8{x}^{2}-2x+6)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8c84cfd58ece715554e171ab022d8fa0e5246d1cf79827113007fe1452df3bc"} {"question" "q-121517" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "45447d29a8e198bb36ec2a1385671367c5029b4bb45c74a95b443ad44df93792"} {"question" "q-121517" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8c84cfd58ece715554e171ab022d8fa0e5246d1cf79827113007fe1452df3bc"} {"question" "q-121517" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-1/4}^{3/4}(8{x}^{2}+x-3)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{3/4}(-4{x}^{2}-x+4)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{3/4}(-8{x}^{2}-2x+6)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-2}^{3/4}(-{x}^{2}+2x-6)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{3/4}^{1}(-8{x}^{2}-3x+2)\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8c84cfd58ece715554e171ab022d8fa0e5246d1cf79827113007fe1452df3bc"} {"question" "q-121517" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-1}^{3/4}(-8{x}^{2}-2x+6)\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8c84cfd58ece715554e171ab022d8fa0e5246d1cf79827113007fe1452df3bc"} {"question" "q-121566" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which integral gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y=2{x}^{2}-3x$ and $y=5-2{x}^{2}-2x?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8c84cfd58ece715554e171ab022d8fa0e5246d1cf79827113007fe1452df3bc"} {"question" "q-121566" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/assets/9a4de823003f0461231c355a43ba9845358ec4a3767ca59c2ba2206456620373.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-3x & =5-2{x}^{2}-2x \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-x-5 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1\\pm \\sqrt{(-1)^{2}-4\\cdot 4\\cdot (-5)}}{2\\cdot 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\pm 9}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-1$ to $x=\\frac{5}{4}.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/assets/94b0113f7ca9ffeca11398cce06995fa4a7e5cb3f39b3021d5d6ec56746b75ee.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=2{x}^{2}-3x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=5-2{x}^{2}-2x.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{5/4}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{5/4}[\\,(5-2{x}^{2}-2x)-(2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{5/4}(-4{x}^{2}+x+5)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8c84cfd58ece715554e171ab022d8fa0e5246d1cf79827113007fe1452df3bc"} {"question" "q-121566" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "45447d29a8e198bb36ec2a1385671367c5029b4bb45c74a95b443ad44df93792"} {"question" "q-121566" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8c84cfd58ece715554e171ab022d8fa0e5246d1cf79827113007fe1452df3bc"} {"question" "q-121566" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{4/5}(4{x}^{2}-x+5)\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{5/4}(-4{x}^{2}+x+5)\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{5/4}(4{x}^{2}-x-5)\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-5/4}^{1}(4{x}^{2}+x-5)\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{1}^{5/4}(-4{x}^{2}-x+5)\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8c84cfd58ece715554e171ab022d8fa0e5246d1cf79827113007fe1452df3bc"} {"question" "q-121566" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-1}^{5/4}(-4{x}^{2}+x+5)\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8c84cfd58ece715554e171ab022d8fa0e5246d1cf79827113007fe1452df3bc"} {"question" "q-121585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the area of the finite region bounded by the curves $y=2{x}^{2}-2$ and $y=6.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8c84cfd58ece715554e171ab022d8fa0e5246d1cf79827113007fe1452df3bc"} {"question" "q-121585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/assets/0fd8a89bdcad9f1b20c9fd35df9aa209a5e551d5d7a7c97f62103e23e7ae9741.png)\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-2 & =6 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-2$ to $x=2.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/assets/83e8d6af5284006b4caad6b1a96cb1298f7c7672d6c35fa652359d3a0b28e037.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=2{x}^{2}-2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=6.$\n\nTherefore, we can find the area, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[\\,(6)-(2{x}^{2}-2)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(8-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3})|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =[8\\cdot 2-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {2}^{3}]-[8\\cdot (-2)-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-2)^{3}] \\\\  & =(16-\\frac{16}{3})-(-16+\\frac{16}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8c84cfd58ece715554e171ab022d8fa0e5246d1cf79827113007fe1452df3bc"} {"question" "q-121585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "45447d29a8e198bb36ec2a1385671367c5029b4bb45c74a95b443ad44df93792"} {"question" "q-121585" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8c84cfd58ece715554e171ab022d8fa0e5246d1cf79827113007fe1452df3bc"} {"question" "q-121585" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{16}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{32}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{62}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{64}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{8}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8c84cfd58ece715554e171ab022d8fa0e5246d1cf79827113007fe1452df3bc"} {"question" "q-121585" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{64}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8c84cfd58ece715554e171ab022d8fa0e5246d1cf79827113007fe1452df3bc"} {"question" "q-121772" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the area of the finite region bounded by the curves $y={x}^{2}-10$ and $y=6.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8c84cfd58ece715554e171ab022d8fa0e5246d1cf79827113007fe1452df3bc"} {"question" "q-121772" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/assets/9248483d8b55c2ac61fcc3c1f11fee0eabee92794eaacd1fb2e37fff68cec289.png)\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-10 & =6 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ x & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-4$ to $x=4.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/assets/173d726e4b0ecee37d8b7ca8878f5bc5934a069dc48a2b9d18e28d72f3b04f7e.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}-10,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=6.$\n\nTherefore, we can find the area, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}[\\,(6)-({x}^{2}-10)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}(16-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(16x-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})|}_{-4}^{4} \\\\  & =[16\\cdot 4-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {4}^{3}]-[16\\cdot (-4)-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-4)^{3}] \\\\  & =(64-\\frac{64}{3})-(-64+\\frac{64}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{256}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8c84cfd58ece715554e171ab022d8fa0e5246d1cf79827113007fe1452df3bc"} {"question" "q-121772" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "45447d29a8e198bb36ec2a1385671367c5029b4bb45c74a95b443ad44df93792"} {"question" "q-121772" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8c84cfd58ece715554e171ab022d8fa0e5246d1cf79827113007fe1452df3bc"} {"question" "q-121772" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "96"] ["math" "\\frac{251}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{256}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{32}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{64}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8c84cfd58ece715554e171ab022d8fa0e5246d1cf79827113007fe1452df3bc"} {"question" "q-121772" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{256}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8c84cfd58ece715554e171ab022d8fa0e5246d1cf79827113007fe1452df3bc"} {"question" "q-356029" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the area of the finite region bounded by the curves $y={x}^{2}-3$ and $y=1.$\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8c84cfd58ece715554e171ab022d8fa0e5246d1cf79827113007fe1452df3bc"} {"question" "q-356029" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/assets/664fcc791ec42b4ec40ed3fc7033fa601d646c9900632864122d31fd01a2cf61.png)\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-3 & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-2$ to $x=2.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/assets/1d10b1994774239767578417bc5f0b74a6a7369005a73fbac859574c9d5d36e0.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}-3,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=1.$\n\nTherefore, we can find the area, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[\\,(1)-({x}^{2}-3)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(4-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(4x-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =[4\\cdot 2-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {2}^{3}]-[4\\cdot (-2)-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-2)^{3}] \\\\  & =(8-\\frac{8}{3})-(-8+\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}-(-\\frac{16}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8c84cfd58ece715554e171ab022d8fa0e5246d1cf79827113007fe1452df3bc"} {"question" "q-356029" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "45447d29a8e198bb36ec2a1385671367c5029b4bb45c74a95b443ad44df93792"} {"question" "q-356029" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8c84cfd58ece715554e171ab022d8fa0e5246d1cf79827113007fe1452df3bc"} {"question" "q-356029" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{32}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8c84cfd58ece715554e171ab022d8fa0e5246d1cf79827113007fe1452df3bc"}] "usage" {} "basis" 1234 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
